Z||000000||HummelHund||09-08-2001||10:12 PM||||I was recently given a tachometer and 120mph speedo out of an 87' GT. While the speedometer works, the tach, when plugged in, reads 3000rpm at idle. Is this tach fixable, or will it make a really cool clock for my desk?<BR>TIA,<P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project/daily driver)<BR>'84 SE (Dad's Project)||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||09-08-2001||10:27 PM||||If you put this tach in on of those 84's, its going to read about 3k.  If you hold up your old tach, you'll see that 3k on the V6 tach is about 1k on the 4cyl tach.  The tachs have the same sweep, but the V6 one starts out at a different point and just goes 500 rpm higher at the top.  What you need to do is swap the circuit boards from your old tach to your new one.  Really easy to do, just a bunch of little nut things to un screw.  Also, you might run into some little push-nut type things, I had to break those off of mine to get it off.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero_hunter85||09-08-2001||10:29 PM||||does the tack move when you rev it up?  and is it a four cylinder or 6 cylinder engine?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||HummelHund||09-09-2001||09:17 AM||||The tach came from an 86' or 87' GT, and was tested in an 87' GT. That bit of info about swapping circuit boards will help when I get to swappin', though, thanks. The tach did rev, we "redlined" it a couple of times (which I think was revving it to about 3000 actual rpm). It seemed to be acting normally, just at 3000rpm instead of around 1000. Does this help any? The tach looks fine, I can't see any corrosion or damage on it.<P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project/daily driver)<BR>'84 SE (Dad's Project)||||reg||14
